From: Chimera
Subject: [PATCH] chimera: the offscreen texture pool survives the frame

submit_frame ended with `self.offscreen_texture_pool = TexturePool::new()`,
which threw the pool away every frame. PoolEntry::drop had just handed each
texture back through a Weak to that pool's `available` vec, so replacing the
pool freed all of them and the next frame's get_texture fell through to the
BufferPool constructor and built them again.

Measured on a GTX 1060 (New Star Soccer, 400 frames, --render-every-frame):
14,074 glGenTextures against 14,031 glDeleteTextures, the counts pairing
almost exactly, each with a glTexStorage2D of its own. On the backends
upstream targets that is cheap. Across a remote GL bridge - where the guest
is sandboxed and every call crosses - it is 1.19 ms of an 18.67 ms frame.

The reset was doing a job, though: a bucket is keyed {size, usage, format,
sample_count}, so a movie whose offscreen sizes vary - hundreds of
cacheAsBitmap children, each its own size - would otherwise accumulate one
bucket per size for the length of the run, which is what the comment about
OOM above the call site is about. So the retention is BOUNDED rather than
unbounded: TexturePool::trim keeps at most `per_bucket` textures in any one
bucket and drops every bucket once there are more than `max_buckets`.

The bound is free at the sizes that matter: trim(64, 64) and trim(4096, 4096)
measure the same (17.48 vs 17.46 ms/frame) with an identical glGenTextures
count, so the cap never binds on real content and the OOM protection costs
nothing.

18.67 -> 17.45 ms/frame (-6.4%), same tree and same build both sides, three
runs each, spread 0.03. Note the win is mostly NOT texture reuse - only 285
of 14,074 creations go away - but the per-frame rebuild of the `pools`
HashMap with a fresh BufferPool and boxed constructor for every key.

Correctness: the core's own gate, all legs, zero failures - image 394/394
(both renderers), state 30/30 (a save and reload before EVERY frame agreeing
on trace, audio and picture), trace/determinism/sandbox 197/197.
diff --git a/render/wgpu/src/backend.rs b/render/wgpu/src/backend.rs
index 3af1ba1b..41e87634 100644
--- a/render/wgpu/src/backend.rs
+++ b/render/wgpu/src/backend.rs
@@ -666,7 +666,14 @@ impl<T: RenderTarget + 'static> RenderBackend for WgpuRenderBackend<T> {
 
         self.active_frame
             .submit_for_target(&self.descriptors, &self.target, frame_output);
-        self.offscreen_texture_pool = TexturePool::new();
+        // This was `= TexturePool::new()`, which freed every texture the frame
+        // had just handed back and made the next frame build them again -
+        // 14,074 glGenTextures against 14,031 glDeleteTextures over 400 frames
+        // on a GTX 1060, each with a glTexStorage2D of its own. They are kept
+        // now, but within a bound, so that a movie with many differently-sized
+        // offscreen caches still cannot grow the pool for the length of the run
+        // (the reason the reset was here at all - see TexturePool::trim).
+        self.offscreen_texture_pool.trim(64, 64);
         self.profiler
             .end_frame()
             .expect("Frame should end successfully");
diff --git a/render/wgpu/src/buffer_pool.rs b/render/wgpu/src/buffer_pool.rs
index 7d36ac70..61d8952b 100644
--- a/render/wgpu/src/buffer_pool.rs
+++ b/render/wgpu/src/buffer_pool.rs
@@ -60,6 +60,44 @@ impl TexturePool {
         pool.take(descriptors, AlwaysCompatible)
     }
 
+    /// Keep the pooled textures for the next frame, within a bound.
+    ///
+    /// The offscreen pool used to be REPLACED after every frame, which threw
+    /// away every texture `PoolEntry::drop` had just handed back: the next
+    /// frame's `get_texture` then found an empty bucket and built a new one.
+    /// Measured on a GTX 1060 (New Star Soccer, 400 frames): 14,074
+    /// glGenTextures against 14,031 glDeleteTextures - the counts pairing
+    /// almost exactly - with a glTexStorage2D apiece, so about thirty-five
+    /// textures were created, given immutable storage, and destroyed every
+    /// frame. On the backends upstream targets that is cheap; across a remote
+    /// GL bridge it is not.
+    ///
+    /// Dropping the pool was doing a job, though, and this keeps it: a bucket
+    /// is keyed by {size, usage, format, sample_count}, so a movie whose
+    /// offscreen sizes vary - hundreds of `cacheAsBitmap` children, each its
+    /// own size - would otherwise accumulate one bucket per size for the whole
+    /// run. That is what the caller's comment about OOM is about. So the
+    /// retention is bounded: at most `per_bucket` textures kept in any one
+    /// bucket, and every bucket dropped once there are more than `max_buckets`
+    /// of them.
+    ///
+    /// The BUCKET COUNT is the axis that can run away - a frame's working set
+    /// is a few dozen textures across a handful of sizes - so the depth cap is
+    /// generous and the bucket cap is what actually bounds a bad movie.
+    pub fn trim(&mut self, per_bucket: usize, max_buckets: usize) {
+        if self.pools.len() > max_buckets {
+            // Nothing here says which bucket is worth keeping, and a bucket
+            // that is still wanted is rebuilt by the next get_texture at the
+            // cost of the textures it held. Clearing all of them is the honest
+            // version of the old behaviour, just far rarer.
+            self.pools.clear();
+            return;
+        }
+        for pool in self.pools.values() {
+            pool.trim(per_bucket);
+        }
+    }
+
     pub fn get_globals(
         &mut self,
         descriptors: &Descriptors,
@@ -139,6 +177,21 @@ impl<Type, Description: BufferDescription> BufferPool<Type, Description> {
         }
     }
 
+    /// Drop all but `keep` of the items waiting in this pool.
+    ///
+    /// Safe against entries that are still out: a `PoolEntry` holds a `Weak`
+    /// to this same `available` vec and pushes itself back on drop whatever
+    /// the vec's length is by then, so a trim can never strand one.
+    pub fn trim(&self, keep: usize) {
+        let mut guard = self
+            .available
+            .lock()
+            .expect("Should not be able to lock recursively");
+        if guard.len() > keep {
+            guard.truncate(keep);
+        }
+    }
+
     pub fn take(
         &self,
         descriptors: &Descriptors,
